### Win32 build notes ###

# Phil Ashby, Dec 2017 - Jan 2018

At present, MSYS2 on a Windows system is required to gather dependencies
before building a Win32 version of gpredict using this build system.

Install MSYS2, open a shell and install the Gtk3 packages,
following the instructions from GTK team:
https://www.gtk.org/download/windows.php

Once your MSYS2 output folder (/mingw32 or similar) is accessible from
this system, adjust the MINGW_ROOT parameter in config.mk to suit.

You should now be able to cross-compile the Win32 version of GPredict
using the 'make' command.

Experimental Win32 build testing is being tracked in this ticket:
https://github.com/csete/gpredict/issues/110


To build a deployable ZIP archive, use 'make dist', which packages all
binary DLLs from dependent packages along with gpredict[-con].exe into
a versioned ZIP.

Good luck!
Phlash.

### Native Windows x64 builds with current MSYS2 ###

Use the MSYS2 UCRT64 shell. Update MSYS2 with
`pacman -Syu`, reopening the shell and repeating if a core update requires it.
Install the build and packaging dependencies:

    pacman -S --needed git make zip unzip mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gcc \
        mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-pkgconf mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gtk3 \
        mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-librsvg mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-adwaita-icon-theme

GTK installs its GLib, GdkPixbuf, Cairo, Pango and other native dependencies.
SVG support requires librsvg explicitly. This Windows Makefile uses WinINet
instead of libcurl and no longer requires GooCanvas, Autotools or Hamlib to
compile. Hamlib's rigctld/rotctld remain separate optional runtime services.

From the repository root:

    cd win32
    make -j"$(nproc)"
    make dist

config.mk selects the active MSYS2 MINGW_PREFIX (/ucrt64 for UCRT64), falling
back to /mingw64 outside MSYS2. Other existing MinGW installations can
still be selected with `make MINGW_ROOT=/path/to/mingw ...`. Version numbers
come from v-prefixed Git tags; with no tags, the release version in NEWS is
used with build number zero. The source commit is recorded separately by CI.
Run `make clean` before changing environments so MSVCRT and UCRT objects/DLLs
are never mixed. The UCRT64 package targets Windows 10/11 x64.
The Makefile uses Gpredict's existing portable strcasestr implementation for
the two Windows objects that call the unavailable GNU function.

`make dist DIST_ARCHIVE=gpredict-2.6-win64.zip` optionally sets the ZIP name;
without this option the historical versioned filename is preserved. The ZIP
contains gpredict.exe, gpredict-con.exe, libsgpsdp.dll, native MinGW runtime
DLLs and GLib helpers (when supplied), image loaders, generated relocatable
loaders.cache, GTK configuration, compiled GLib schemas, the complete Adwaita
and hicolor themes (including AdwaitaLegacy when installed), dependency
licenses, application data and maps.
The checked-in loaders.cache is retained for reference; current distributions
generate their cache from the installed loaders rather than reuse it.
Extract the entire ZIP and run gpredict/gpredict.exe. No MSYS2 installation
is required. User settings continue to use Gpredict's existing user directory.

### GitHub Actions ###

Every push to master runs the build and publishes a verified release.
Actions -> Windows x64 Build -> Run workflow also allows manual builds.
The workflow uses the
standard windows-2022 runner, a fresh MSYS2 UCRT64 environment, and the same
`make` / `make dist` targets. For the current release, the artifact
`gpredict-windows-x64` contains `gpredict-2.6-win64.zip`, SHA256SUMS, the source
commit and the complete installed package version list.

Successful push and manual runs also publish the same files to GitHub Releases, using
the workflow's GITHUB_TOKEN with contents: write permission. The tag is
windows-v<version>-build.<run-number> (for example windows-v2.6-build.3), targeting
the exact source commit. Each new run creates a separate release;
rerunning the same run updates its assets. Release publication happens
automatically after all portable checks
and the artifact upload succeed, and its URL appears in the run summary.
The windows- prefix keeps these tags outside config.mk's upstream version
tag matching, so subsequent builds retain valid numeric resource versions.

Before upload, CI tests ZIP integrity, extracts to a path containing spaces,
checks every executable/DLL for x64 and missing imports (including transitive
loader dependencies), rejects MSYS/Cygwin dependencies, checks data, schemas
and relative PNG/JPEG/SVG loader paths, decodes example PNG/JPEG/SVG files
using a temporary native GdkPixbuf tool, and runs gpredict-con.exe --help from
outside the package with only Windows system directories on PATH. The image
test tool is not included in the ZIP.

MSYS2 is a rolling distribution: this is a repeatable build procedure with
recorded inputs, not a byte-for-byte reproducible build across different
dates. Dependency versions, action tags and ZIP timestamps can change.
The startup check does not replace interactive testing of maps, icons,
network updates, radio/rotator services or a clean Windows machine.
The existing Windows translation configuration is preserved.
